cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use DeepCatch in your research, please cite it as follows."
title: "DeepCatch v2.3.0: An Open-Source Benchmark for cfDNA Fragmentomics with External Validation on Held-Out Public Cohorts"
authors:
  - given-names: "Yu Ching"
    family-names: "Lam"
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher (no institutional affiliation; verified email domain umich.edu)"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0008-9113-769X"
year: 2026
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.to-be-assigned-on-first-release"
url: "https://github.com/rollroyces/deepcatch"
repository-code: "https://github.com/rollroyces/deepcatch"
repository-companion: "https://github.com/rollroyces/cfdna-fragmentomics-pipeline"
license: MIT
version: "2.3.0"
date-released: 2026-11-16
keywords:
  - "cfDNA"
  - "ctDNA"
  - "liquid biopsy"
  - "molecular residual disease (MRD)"
  - "panel LLR"
  - "fragmentomics"
  - "tumor-naive detection"
  - "open science"
  - "benchmark"
abstract: >
  DeepCatch v2.3.0 is an open-source cfDNA fragmentomics benchmark that is
  validated on public data the model has never been trained on. A 5-channel
  DELFI profile (5 Mb / 100 kb ratios and coverage, plus a 196-bin fragment-size
  distribution) is extracted from publicly served FinaleDB fragment files and
  scored with a per-study harmonised PCA + logistic-regression pipeline.
  Training on FinaleDB publications 6 + 8 (n=627) and testing on held-out
  publications 1, 7 and 9 gives an external AUC of 0.9915 [0.9712, 1.0000] on
  the whole-genome publication (n=31) and 0.8819 [0.7701, 0.9936] pooled over
  the publications carrying both classes; the whole-exome publication does not
  transfer (breast 0.4119, prostate 0.3193), so the assay platform, not the
  study, is the binding constraint. A leave-one-study-out run inside the
  training cohort gives 0.6805, so the pooled 0.9670 is reported as a
  within-cohort separation number rather than a generalisation claim. The
  multi-modal stage is carried by a regularized late fusion (AUC 0.9825) and a
  Bayesian model average (0.9650) rather than the neural encoder (0.554) at
  this sample size. The methylation path runs end to end on real public
  fragment data. Not clinically validated; research use only.
